= Solution
{parent=i}

The defining square for the <subobject classifier> is a <pullback in a category>:
$$
\begin{array}{ccc}A&\longrightarrow&1\\\downarrow&&\downarrow\top_{\mathcal E}\\X&\xrightarrow{a}&\Omega_{\mathcal E}.\end{array}
$$
The <inverse image functor of a geometric morphism> preserves this square and the terminal object. By definition, the inverse-image truth arrow $f^*\top_{\mathcal E}$ is itself the pullback of $\top_{\mathcal F}$ along $\bar\lambda$. Composing these two pullback squares shows that $f^*A\hookrightarrow f^*X$ is the truth pullback along $\bar\lambda\circ f^*a$. Uniqueness of the characteristic map gives
$$
\boxed{\chi_{f^*A}=\bar\lambda\circ f^*a}.
$$

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take two <subobjects> $A,B\hookrightarrow X$ whose inverse images coincide. Their characteristic maps $a,b:X\to\Omega_{\mathcal E}$ satisfy $\bar\lambda f^*a=\bar\lambda f^*b$ by part (i). Under the <adjunction> $f^*\dashv f_*$, these maps transpose to $\lambda a$ and $\lambda b$, respectively, by naturality of the unit. If $\lambda$ is a <monomorphism>, then $a=b$ and hence $A=B$. Thus $f^*$ reflects equality of subobjects.

For parallel arrows $r,s:X\rightrightarrows Y$ with $f^*r=f^*s$, their <equalizer> $K\hookrightarrow X$ pulls back to all of $f^*X$. Reflection of equality with the whole subobject gives $K=X$, so $r=s$. Therefore **the inverse image is faithful, and the geometric morphism is surjective**. This is the <surjectivity detected by the subobject classifier> criterion. For completeness, faithfulness is equivalent here to conservativity: if $f^*h$ is invertible, the image inclusion of $h$ and its kernel-pair diagonal become whole subobjects; reflection makes $h$ both a regular epimorphism and monic, hence invertible in the <topos>.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Suppose $f$ is a <surjective geometric morphism>, so $f^*$ is faithful. First, if a <monomorphism> $m:S\hookrightarrow X$ becomes an isomorphism, then its characteristic map $\chi_m$ and the constantly true map become equal after $f^*$: by part (i), the pulled-back subobject is the whole object, and equivalently $f^*\chi_m=f^*\top_X$ because $f^*m$ is epic and these maps agree after precomposition with it. Faithfulness gives $\chi_m=\top_X$, so $m$ was already invertible.

Now let $a,b:X\to\Omega_{\mathcal E}$ satisfy $\lambda a=\lambda b$. Adjunction and part (i) identify their pulled-back subobjects $f^*A=f^*B$. Each inclusion $A\cap B\hookrightarrow A,B$ consequently becomes an isomorphism. By the preceding argument it already is an isomorphism, so $A=B$ and $a=b$. This works for every test object $X$, proving
$$
\boxed{\lambda:\Omega_{\mathcal E}\to f_*\Omega_{\mathcal F}\text{ is monic}.}
$$

