= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Identify the <reflective subcategory> with its full replete image, writing $\eta_X:X\to LX$ for the reflection unit. An object $Z$ is fixed by $L$ exactly when precomposition with every $\eta_X$ gives a bijection $\mathcal E(LX,Z)\to\mathcal E(X,Z)$. One implication is the reflection <adjunction>; for the other, take $X=Z$ to obtain a retraction of $\eta_Z$, and uniqueness of maps into the fixed object $LZ$ makes it a two-sided inverse.

An ambient <finite limit> of fixed objects has this same property: its <hom-sets> are limits of the hom-sets of the diagram, and a limit of these bijections is a bijection. Thus the ambient terminal object, products and <equalizers> are fixed and retain their universal properties in the full subcategory. **The reflective subcategory is Cartesian, with finite limits computed in the ambient topos.**

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $A,B$ be fixed objects and form the ambient <exponential object> $B^A$. For every $X$, the exponential <adjunction> identifies maps $X\to B^A$ with maps $X\times A\to B$. Since $L$ is left exact and $LA=A$,
$$
L(X\times A)\cong LX\times A,
$$
and its unit corresponds to $\eta_X\times1_A$. Because $B$ is fixed, every map $X\times A\to B$ therefore extends uniquely to $LX\times A\to B$. Transposing back gives
$$
\mathcal E(LX,B^A)\cong\mathcal E(X,B^A).
$$
The fixed-object criterion from part (i) shows that $B^A$ already belongs to $\mathcal L$. The ambient evaluation and transpose universal property restrict to the full subcategory, proving **the subcategory is cartesian closed**. This constructs <exponentials in a left-exact reflective subcategory>, rather than merely reflecting an exponential and assuming its universal property survives.

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a <subobject> $m:S\hookrightarrow X$, define the <closure operation induced by a left-exact reflector> by
$$
\boxed{c_X(S)=X\times_{LX}LS\hookrightarrow X},
$$
where the pullback uses $\eta_X:X\to LX$ and the monomorphism $Lm:LS\hookrightarrow LX$. Naturality of the unit factors $S\hookrightarrow X$ through $c_X(S)$, proving extensivity, and functoriality of $L$ proves monotonicity. Applying the left-exact $L$ to the defining pullback gives $L(c_X(S))\cong LS$, since $L\eta_X$ is invertible. Repeating the construction therefore changes nothing, proving idempotence.

For $h:Y\to X$, naturality of $\eta$ and preservation of pullbacks identify the closure of $h^*S$ with $Y\times_{LX}LS$, which is also $h^*c_X(S)$. Hence
$$
c_Y(h^*S)=h^*c_X(S).
$$
**The operation is thus universal: it is stable under every pullback.** The same construction preserves finite intersections because $L$ is left exact.

= iv
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Every characteristic map $a:X\to\Omega$ expresses its <subobject> as $a^*\top$. Universality of the <closure operation induced by a left-exact reflector> gives
$$
c_X(a^*\top)=a^*c_\Omega(\top).
$$
By definition, $j$ classifies $c_\Omega(\top)$, so the right-hand subobject has characteristic map $j\circ a$. The original and its closure agree exactly when their unique characteristic maps agree. Thus
$$
\boxed{a\text{ classifies a closed subobject}\Longleftrightarrow j\circ a=a.}
$$
Extensivity, idempotence, preservation of intersections and closure of the whole subobject also give the <Lawvere-Tierney topology> laws for $j$.

= v
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Let $\Omega_j\hookrightarrow\Omega$ be the <equalizer> of $j$ and the identity. By part (iv), maps $X\to\Omega_j$ correspond exactly to closed <subobjects> of $X$. Closed subobjects of $X$ in turn correspond to <subobjects> of $LX$ in $\mathcal L$: send $S$ to $LS\hookrightarrow LX$, and in the reverse direction pull a fixed subobject $N\hookrightarrow LX$ back along $\eta_X$. Left exactness shows that these operations are inverse; the pulled-back object has reflection $N$, making it closed by the defining formula.

Consequently
$$
\mathcal E(X,\Omega_j)\cong\operatorname{Sub}_{\mathcal L}(LX).
$$
Replacing $X$ by $LX$ and using $L\eta_X$ invertible shows that maps into $\Omega_j$ extend uniquely across every unit. The criterion of part (i) therefore proves $\Omega_j$ itself is fixed, so belongs to $\mathcal L$. Truth factors through it because $j\top=\top$. For fixed $X$, the displayed bijection becomes
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L(X,\Omega_j)\cong\operatorname{Sub}_{\mathcal L}(X)}.
$$
With the factored truth arrow this is precisely the <subobject classifier> universal property. Thus the <closed-subobject classifier> supplies the classifier in the reflective subcategory.

